I published [a k3s platform kit](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/) with the bill attached,
and I run [a production app on one FreeBSD box](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for
about €7 a month. Both are mine. They disagree.

For the workload most people have — a handful of services, one operator, a
private network — is a three node k3s cluster better than three
FreeBSD boxes running jails behind a load balancer? I do not think it is. The
crossover is much further out than the industry default assumes — and past it,
FreeBSD loses badly, for reasons that are not the usual ones.

::: note
**Part 4 of 7 of [Where to start with self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about self-hosting a platform, and about whether you should.

Previous: [The edge of a self-hosted cluster](/blog/the-edge-of-a-self-hosted-cluster/)

